      "sequence": [
        {
          "segmentId": "types",
          "phaseId": "contract",
          "importance": "critical",
          "prose": "Start at the contract. The feature is a data shape: **segments** are order-independent slices of the diff, and **orders** are reading views over them. A segment can lead one order and sit in another's `rest` — that single split is what makes *key-changes-first* and *results-first* two views of one dataset. Everything downstream serves this type."
        },
        {
          "segmentId": "validator",
          "phaseId": "boundary",
          "importance": "normal",
          "prose": "Because the JSON is authored *outside* Codiff, this module is the trust boundary. `normalizeNarrativeWalkthrough` drops stops referencing unknown segments, removes segments whose path isn't in the diff, and **repairs each `anchor.sectionId`** against the live sections. The renderer never sees a dangling reference."
        },
        {
          "segmentId": "schema",
          "phaseId": "author",
          "importance": "normal",
          "prose": "The committed JSON schema is the human-readable face of the same contract — the `$schema` URL authoring agents point at, every field documented so an editor validates as you write."
        },
        {
          "segmentId": "skill",
          "phaseId": "author",
          "importance": "normal",
          "prose": "The `/walkthrough` skill is where the JSON gets written — by the agent, in-session, because it already holds the conversation. This very document was produced by running it on this PR."
        },
        {
          "segmentId": "cli-args",
          "phaseId": "wiring",
          "importance": "context",
          "prose": "Now the plumbing that lets a file reach Codiff. The bin CLI grows a `--walkthrough-file` flag, parsed alongside the existing `--walkthrough-context`."
        },
        {
          "segmentId": "cli-parse",
          "phaseId": "wiring",
          "importance": "context",
          "prose": "The electron side parses the same flag (and the `CODIFF_WALKTHROUGH_FILE` env var) into `launchOptions.walkthroughFile`."
        },
        {
          "segmentId": "ipc",
          "phaseId": "wiring",
          "importance": "normal",
          "prose": "The renderer reaches the document through this IPC handler: read the file, run it through the normalizer against the current diff, return `ready`/`unavailable` — or `null` when no file was supplied, so the UI can fall back to the agent walkthrough."
        },
        {
          "segmentId": "session",
          "phaseId": "wiring",
          "importance": "context",
          "prose": "The seam the UI hangs on: an optional `narrativeWalkthrough` field on `SourceSession`, saved and restored per source."
        },
        {
          "segmentId": "viewmodel",
          "phaseId": "interface",
          "importance": "normal",
          "prose": "Into the interface. `buildOrderView` is the pure heart: it resolves each stop's segment, indexes the sequence, fills phases with their stops, and groups the rest by reason. It's unit-tested with no React in sight."
        },
        {
          "segmentId": "nav",
          "phaseId": "interface",
          "importance": "normal",
          "prose": "The shared navigation state — active order, focused index, stop-vs-rest, and the visited set keyed by segment id. App owns this hook so a click in the sidebar and a click in the arc move the same cursor."
        },
        {
          "segmentId": "mainview",
          "phaseId": "interface",
          "importance": "critical",
          "prose": "The view you're reading this in. The order toggle, the metro-map arc, the focused stop, the rest overview, and the full-context reader — and crucially, each stop's diff is the **real `ReviewCodeView`, scoped to one file**, so highlighting and inline comments are the genuine article, not a mock."
        },
        {
          "segmentId": "sidebar",
          "phaseId": "interface",
          "importance": "normal",
          "prose": "The narrative table of contents that replaced the file tree: phases as chapters with progress, stop rows with a rail and a tick, and the muted “the rest” group. Driven by the same navigation hook."
